<h2>A prosecution that outlived its marriage</h2>
<p>The underlying case — P.S. Case No. 47 of 2007, Laheriyasarai, Darbhanga — was instituted by the wife alleging cruelty over dowry demands against the husband and four in-laws. The marriage it concerned ended on 21 January 2010, when the Family Court at Patna dissolved it by mutual consent under Section 13-B of the Hindu Marriage Act. Yet the criminal case marched on: cognizance, a charge-framing order of 14 February 2019, and a failed revision before the Sessions Court. By the time the quashing petition was decided, the prosecution had outlived the marriage by more than sixteen years.</p>

<h2>The legal framework</h2>
<p>Justice Chandra Shekhar Jha applied the two lines of authority that govern this terrain. The <em>Bhajan Lal</em> categories mark the outer boundaries of the inherent power; and the Supreme Court\'s decision in <em>Abhishek v. State of Madhya Pradesh</em> (2023) — together with the broader <em>Gian Singh</em> principle — recognises that matrimonial offences, though formally non-compoundable, are quintessentially personal, and that forcing a settled dispute to trial is itself an abuse of the court\'s process.</p>

<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>The formalist view</h3><p>Charge has been framed, a revision has failed, and Section 498A is non-compoundable — so the trial must run to verdict regardless of the settlement.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>The view the Court took</h3><p>The parties divorced by consent in 2010, performed their settlement — custody, maintenance, property — and have no surviving dispute. A trial now would consume judicial time to no end a conviction could serve.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What practitioners should take from it</h2>
<div class="check">
<p><strong>For accused families in legacy matrimonial cases:</strong> a failed revision against charge does not foreclose a settlement-based quashing — the two proceed on different questions. Collect the complete settlement record: the compromise petition, the decree, proof that its terms (alimony, custody, property) were performed, and any recital that criminal cases would be withdrawn. Where the complainant has moved on and cannot be served, that record becomes the case. And act early: every year a dead prosecution stays alive, witnesses scatter and costs compound.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Trial courts cannot quash; only the High Court\'s inherent power reaches a settled-but-pending prosecution. The decision is a reminder that the power exists precisely for cases like this one — where the criminal docket carries a dispute that the parties themselves buried sixteen years ago. The order quashing the charge of 14 February 2019 and all consequential proceedings closed the last open file of a marriage that ended in 2010.</p>
</div>
